</section>
<div class="ltx_pagination ltx_role_newpage"></div>
<section id="A1" class="ltx_appendix">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_appendix"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_appendix">Appendix A </span>Effect of Top-<math id="A1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math> Choice in Word Recovery Decoding</h2>

<div id="A1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In the main experiments, we measure word recovery by decoding the top-<math id="A1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math> predicted tokens from each character-level hidden state and aggregating them across the sequence. This choice introduces a potential sensitivity to the value of <math id="A1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math>: smaller <math id="A1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math> values provide a stricter notion of recoverability, while larger <math id="A1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math> values may increase recall at the cost of including less confident predictions.</p>
</div>
<div id="A1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">To assess the robustness of our findings to this design choice, we repeat the word recovery analysis using a range of values <math id="A1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K\in\{1,2,3,5,10,20\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>3</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>5</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>10</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>20</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K\in\{1,2,3,5,10,20\}</annotation></semantics></math>. Across all models and datasets, we find that the qualitative behavior of word recovery remains consistent (Figure <a href="#A2.F8" title="Figure 8 ‣ Appendix B Additional Analysis: In-Group Word Recovery Decoding ‣ Word Recovery in Large Language Models Enables Character-Level Tokenization Robustness" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">8</span></a>, Figure <a href="#A2.F9" title="Figure 9 ‣ Appendix B Additional Analysis: In-Group Word Recovery Decoding ‣ Word Recovery in Large Language Models Enables Character-Level Tokenization Robustness" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">9</span></a>, Figure <a href="#A2.F10" title="Figure 10 ‣ Appendix B Additional Analysis: In-Group Word Recovery Decoding ‣ Word Recovery in Large Language Models Enables Character-Level Tokenization Robustness" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">10</span></a>). In particular, the layerwise recovery curves exhibit similar shapes and relative ordering across models, and the same early-vs.-late recovery patterns described in the main text persist for all tested values of <math id="A1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="A1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">While increasing <math id="A1.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math> naturally leads to higher absolute recovery scores, the onset layer at which recovery begins and the saturation behavior across layers are largely unchanged. This indicates that our conclusions about the emergence and dynamics of word recovery do not depend on a specific choice of <math id="A1.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math>. Based on this analysis, we use <math id="A1.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K=5" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>5</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K=5</annotation></semantics></math> in the main experiments as a balance between strictness and robustness.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A2" class="ltx_appendix">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_appendix"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_appendix">Appendix B </span>Additional Analysis: In-Group Word Recovery Decoding</h2>

<div id="A2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In the main analysis, we define word recovery using a set-based decoding formulation that aggregates top-<math id="A2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math> predictions across the entire character sequence (Section <a href="#S3" title="3 Detecting Word Recovery in Hidden States ‣ Word Recovery in Large Language Models Enables Character-Level Tokenization Robustness" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>). While this formulation captures whether canonical lexical identities are present anywhere in the hidden states, it may overestimate recovery if a token is predicted spuriously at positions unrelated to its original character span. To address this concern, we conduct a more restrictive decoding analysis that limits recovery to <em id="A2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">in-group character ranges</em>. Specifically, for each canonical token <math id="A2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> with character span <math id="A2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{G}(t_{i})=[s_{i},e_{i})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒢</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>e</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{G}(t_{i})=[s_{i},e_{i})</annotation></semantics></math>, we restrict decoding to hidden states within this span. Let</p>
<table id="A2.Ex10" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A2.Ex10.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{P}^{(\ell)}_{\text{group}}(t_{i})=\bigcup_{j=s_{i}}^{e_{i}-1}\mathrm{TopK}(p^{(\ell)}_{j})," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒫</mi><mtext>group</mtext><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">⋃</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>e</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></munderover><mrow><mi>TopK</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>p</mi><mi>j</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{P}^{(\ell)}_{\text{group}}(t_{i})=\bigcup_{j=s_{i}}^{e_{i}-1}\mathrm{TopK}(p^{(\ell)}_{j}),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A2.p1.2" class="ltx_p">and we say that <math id="A2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> is recovered at layer <math id="A2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ell" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ell</annotation></semantics></math> under in-group decoding if <math id="A2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t_{i}\in\mathcal{P}^{(\ell)}_{\text{group}}(t_{i})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒫</mi><mtext>group</mtext><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t_{i}\in\mathcal{P}^{(\ell)}_{\text{group}}(t_{i})</annotation></semantics></math>. The corresponding in-group recovery score is defined as</p>
<table id="A2.Ex11" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A2.Ex11.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="R^{(\ell)}_{\text{group}}=\frac{1}{|\mathcal{T}|}\sum_{t_{i}\in\mathcal{T}}\mathbb{I}\big[t_{i}\in\mathcal{P}^{(\ell)}_{\text{group}}(t_{i})\big]." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>R</mi><mtext>group</mtext><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mfrac><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi></mrow></munder><mi>𝕀</mi><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">[</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒫</mi><mtext>group</mtext><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">]</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R^{(\ell)}_{\text{group}}=\frac{1}{|\mathcal{T}|}\sum_{t_{i}\in\mathcal{T}}\mathbb{I}\big[t_{i}\in\mathcal{P}^{(\ell)}_{\text{group}}(t_{i})\big].</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="A2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">This formulation ensures that a canonical token is counted as recovered only if its identity is decodable from the hidden states of its own character span, rather than from unrelated positions elsewhere in the sequence.</p>
</div>
<div id="A2.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Across all models and datasets, we find that in-group decoding yields slightly lower absolute recovery scores than the set-based formulation, as expected under the stricter criterion (Figure <a href="#A2.F11" title="Figure 11 ‣ Appendix B Additional Analysis: In-Group Word Recovery Decoding ‣ Word Recovery in Large Language Models Enables Character-Level Tokenization Robustness" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">11</span></a>). However, the qualitative conclusions remain unchanged. In particular, (i) word recovery still emerges reliably across layers under character-level tokenization, (ii) the relative ordering of models and datasets is preserved, and (iii) the layerwise recovery dynamics, early saturation in Gemma-2-9B-It and two-stage recovery in Qwen2.5-7B-Instruct and Llama-3.2-3B-Instruct, remain consistent.</p>
</div>
<div id="A2.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.p4.1" class="ltx_p">These results indicate that the main findings are not driven by spurious global decoding effects. Instead, recovered word-level representations are localized to the character spans from which they originate, supporting the interpretation of word recovery as a genuine internal aggregation process rather than an artifact of set-based decoding.</p>
</div>
<figure id="A2.F8" class="ltx_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_4">
<figure id="A2.F8.sf1" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel ltx_align_center"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2603.10771v1/topk_g_d1.svg" id="A2.F8.sf1.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_square" style="aspect-ratio:476/384;" width="476" height="384"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="A2.F8.sf1.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">(a)</span> </span><span id="A2.F8.sf1.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">ARC-E</span></figcaption>
</figure></div><div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_4">
<figure id="A2.F8.sf2" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel ltx_align_center"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2603.10771v1/topk_g_d2.svg" id="A2.F8.sf2.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_square" style="aspect-ratio:476/384;" width="476" height="384"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="A2.F8.sf2.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">(b)</span> </span><span id="A2.F8.sf2.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">ARC-C</span></figcaption>
</figure></div><div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_4">
<figure id="A2.F8.sf3" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel ltx_align_center"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2603.10771v1/topk_g_d3.svg" id="A2.F8.sf3.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_square" style="aspect-ratio:476/384;" width="476" height="384"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="A2.F8.sf3.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">(c)</span> </span><span id="A2.F8.sf3.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">CSQA</span></figcaption>
</figure></div><div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_4">
<figure id="A2.F8.sf4" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel ltx_align_center"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2603.10771v1/topk_g_d4.svg" id="A2.F8.sf4.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_square" style="aspect-ratio:476/384;" width="476" height="384"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="A2.F8.sf4.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">(d)</span> </span><span id="A2.F8.sf4.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">OpenbookQA</span></figcaption>
</figure></div></div>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="A2.F8.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Figure 8</span>: </span><span id="A2.F8.5" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">
<span id="A2.F8.5.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Effect of top-<math id="A2.F8.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math> choice on word recovery for Gemma-2-9B-It.</span>
We report layerwise word recovery scores under character-level tokenization for different values of <math id="A2.F8.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K\in\{1,2,3,5,10,20\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.110em" minsize="1.110em">{</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>3</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>5</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>10</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>20</mn></mrow><mo maxsize="1.110em" minsize="1.110em">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K\in\{1,2,3,5,10,20\}</annotation></semantics></math> across four datasets. While larger <math id="A2.F8.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math> yields uniformly higher absolute recovery scores, the overall layerwise trends and relative recovery dynamics remain consistent across choices of <math id="A2.F8.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math>, indicating that our findings are not sensitive to the specific value of <math id="A2.F8.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></figcaption>
</figure>
<figure id="A2.F9" class="ltx_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_4">
<figure id="A2.F9.sf1" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel ltx_align_center"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2603.10771v1/topk_q_d1.svg" id="A2.F9.sf1.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_square" style="aspect-ratio:476/384;" width="476" height="384"></object>
